notes:Execution base: continue from accepted curator-spec candidate /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-2kp3tv/curator-spec-worktree and create a task-owned worktree/copy; preserve that predecessor read-only and do not stage, commit, publish, advance release pins, or claim platform validation. Define one closed v1 contract shared by local vendored and external-repository builds for Go, Rust, Swift, and Kotlin: manifest declares manager-defined toolchain id plus an explicit version constraint/compatibility requirement; manager policy alone resolves trusted executable paths and primary-source/platform remediation guidance. Package/repository data must not supply executable paths, download URLs, channels, package-manager/install commands, environment overrides, or trust roots. Host availability/version preflight must run before source acquisition or persistent mutation; source metadata compatibility cross-check runs only after local source validation or exact external acquisition+audit and before compiler work. Missing/unparseable/incompatible toolchain is an installation error with stable diagnostics and manager-owned recommendations; v1 never auto-installs. Resolved executable identity/version/target fingerprint must bind cache, receipt, marker/currentness semantics without changing frozen released artifacts. Include normative ordering, schemas/registry, positive+negative vectors, deterministic generation and release gates; keep skill-build.json as the sole neutral external descriptor.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-af7289, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260728-af7289)\nDelivered decision 0007 (canonical) plus docs/compiled-build-toolchain-requirements.md (implementation-ready reference) and one CHANGELOG bullet, in task worktree /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-1g0z69/curator-spec-worktree. Predecessor TASK-260728-2kp3tv worktree preserved read-only (still 127 dirty entries at 57c1f56). Nothing staged, committed, published, pinned; no platform validation claimed.\n\nContract: toolchain-registry-v1 maps each driver to one primary toolchain plus companions over the closed id set go/rust/swift/kotlin/jdk; go entry complete, rust/swift/kotlin/jdk reserved with a fixed obligation list their own driver decisions must fill on a qualified host. toolchain-requirement-v1 is exactly {id, version} with exact/at_least/range over a canonical major.minor.patch triple; combination is interval intersection (associative, commutative, source-order-independent) and an empty intersection is rejected before the host is probed. Prereleases satisfy nothing and cannot appear in a requirement, because no cross-language prerelease order exists. Resolution admits only manager-bundled or trusted operator config; the forbidden-origin and package-influence lists are exhaustive. Two-stage preflight: Stage A (availability/version/platform) right after manifest parse, before external acquisition, cache lookup and persistent mutation; Stage B (go.mod / Cargo.toml + rust-toolchain.toml / swift-tools-version / selected Kotlin field) after local validation or exact external acquisition and audit, before any cache candidate read or compiler child. Metadata is an assertion, never a selector. Eleven stable build_toolchain_* codes carry a guidance_id only; toolchain-guidance-catalog-v1 is manager-owned, versioned, total (release-gate enforced), primary-source-only, text+URL never a command. No auto-install in v1, named explicitly for rustup/swiftly/sdkman/asdf/mise/Homebrew/winget/Gradle wrapper/GOTOOLCHAIN.\n\nKey load-bearing property: a version constraint can only filter the manager-trusted set, never add a candidate. Effective requirement is a gate, not a build input (same reasoning that excluded capability evidence in decision 0006); no bypass because Stage A precedes cache lookup. Result: zero frozen artifacts change and adopting the contract causes no rebuild. Go costs no new process - Stage A reuses the three existing package-independent bootstrap probes, so the five Go argument-vector forms stay five and manager-worker-v1 is untouched.\n\nStated asymmetry: a descriptor requirement is unreadable at Stage A because the descriptor arrives with the repository, so Stage A gates on baseline INTERSECT manifest and the descriptor requirement joins at Stage B. That is why the consuming-manifest requirement is REQUIRED in the next schema.\n\nGates, standalone, real exit codes - validate 0 (42 schemas / 422 vector files, before and after), python unittest 0 (29 tests), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, git diff --check 0, generate-vectors 0. Regenerated conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json are byte-identical to the accepted predecessor. Clean git-init probe: regenerate-check x2 = 0, release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 = 0. Two negative probes expected-red and red: retired descriptor stem in the new doc rejected by validate (exit 1), broken local link in decision 0007 rejected by validate (exit 1); both reverted, final validate 0. Note: make regenerate-check cannot pass inside the task worktree because it diffs against 57c1f56 while the rc.5 candidate is uncommitted - determinism proven by predecessor byte-comparison and the clean probe instead.\n\nDeliberately not done: no protocol/profile/SECURITY/schema/vector change (TASK-260728-2jaw7h), no schema version numbers (TASK-260728-2spy93), no README release-table row (would falsely claim the design is part of the rc.5 specification set), and no invented probe argv or normalization for rust/swift/kotlin/jdk (needs a qualified host).\n\nReviewer focus: is the filter-never-select claim airtight across manifest, descriptor and every stage-B metadata source; is Stage A movement genuinely gate-neutral; is excluding the effective requirement from the cache key correct given Stage A precedes lookup; are the reserved obligation lists complete enough that downstream driver decisions cannot reopen selection; does prerelease rejection break a case worth admitting.\nagent completed: [implementer] developer (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260728-af7289, pid=8002, exit=0)\nReviewer focus: treat this as the research/design contract whose normative schema/vector landing is explicitly TASK-260728-2jaw7h. Independently challenge whether constraints can only filter manager-trusted candidates; Stage A ordering is truly before acquisition/cache/mutation without dropping required predecessors; Stage B cannot bypass source validation/audit; descriptor asymmetry is fail-closed; prerelease/version intersection is deterministic; package data cannot select path/URL/channel/install/env/trust; guidance is manager-owned primary-source text/URL only and v1 cannot auto-install. Verify whether excluding the effective requirement and guidance catalog from cache identity is sound while resolved toolchain identities remain bound, and whether reserved Rust/Swift/Kotlin/JDK obligations are sufficient for downstream work. Re-run docs/link/retired-name and unchanged rc.5 release gates; no stage, commit, publish, pin advance, or platform claim.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-e30a89, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260728-e30a89)
